{{date|September 16, 2008}}

[[Image:Lehman Brothers Times Square by David Shankbone.jpg|thumb|right|Lehman Brothers’ headquarters in New York City. {{Image source|[[User:David Shankbone|David Shankbone]]}}]]
[[w:Lehman Brothers|Lehman Brothers]], a major [[United States|American]] [[w:investment banking|investment banker]], has filed paperwork for bankruptcy. It would be the largest collapse of an investment firm in 18 years.

Lehman attempted to find a buyer over the weekend but it met with no success. The last remaining bidder, [[w:Barclays plc|Barclays PLC]] bank pulled out Sunday afternoon. Barclays, a [[United Kingdom]]-based bank, had become the sole bidder after a consortium led by [[w:Bank of America|Bank of America]] pulled out early Sunday morning citing that it would need government support before considering a bid. The support would consist of backing bad debts owed by Lehman. Barclays cited a similar reason for withdrawing its bid.

As of Monday morning, no buyer has come through to take control of the firm. As a result, the firm filed for [[w:Chapter 11, Title 11, United States Code|bankruptcy protection]]. 

{{HYS question left|Should government money be used in helping support Lehman Brothers?}}
The main sticking point for potential buyers appears to be the unwillingness for the U.S. government to provide financial support. [[w:Hank Paulson|Hank Paulson]], [[w:Treasury Department|Treasury secretary]], has repeatedly said that no government money will be given to Lehman or used in any takeover the firm. Government money was used in the takeover of [[w:Bear Stearns|Bear Stearns]] by [[w:JPMorgan Chase|JPMorgan Chase]] in March of this year. Bear Stearns suffered similar problems as Lehman.

A spokesmen for Barclays told the ''Financial Times'', "the proposed transaction required a guarantee for the trading operations of Lehman Brothers that was potentially open-ended, and we were not willing to provide that guarantee."

Paulson, along with other government officials, proposed a solution that Lehman would get split into two separate entities. However, this proposal has been discarded by many of [[w:Wall Street|Wall Street]] banks. 

Members of the [[w:G8|G8]] have reportedly been keep abreast of the situation by U.S. government. 

The firm, which has over 25,000 staffers, has suffered bad results due to the [[w:subprime mortgage crisis|subprime mortgage crisis]].

